.TH CHMAP_GET_PTR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_get_ptr \- get pointer to value by key (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

value_ptr = chmap_get_ptr(hm_name, key);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for chmap_get_elem_ref(). It returns a pointer to the
value. It returns NULL when the map does not hold the key. It does not
stop the program for a key that is not there. chmap_get() does stop.

The lookup key goes through the DECLARED key type of the map, in the
same way as in chmap_insert(3). A key that the map stored through that
conversion is only ever found again by a lookup that makes the same
one. A literal and a computed expression are both accepted.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Hash map to search
key      Key to look up
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to value if key found
Returns NULL if key not found
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Returns NULL when the map does not hold the key (it does not stop the
program)
Returns NULL when key is a NULL character pointer, which is not a
string
Stops the program when the size of the value does not match the size
of the type
Returns a pointer to the value, so that the caller can change the
value in place
The pointer is correctly aligned for the type of the value. This is
true for both internal backends
A later insert, delete or resize invalidates the pointer
For a char* value type, the target of the pointer is const
(char *const *). The caller can read the stored string. The caller can
also edit its bytes in place, inside the stored length. But a
replacement of the pointer itself is a compile error. The map owns the
string bytes and keeps its own accessor in step with them. A
replacement of the pointer therefore leaves the length of the accessor
describing the earlier string. Call chmap_insert(3) to replace a string
value. It frees the old bytes and copies the new ones into storage that
the map owns
For a float or double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's
key equality contract (bitwise, except -0.0 canonicalized to 0.0)
For a long double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's key
equality contract (value-based, unlike every other key type)
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, int);
int key = 42, val = 100;
chmap_insert(map, key, val);
int* ptr = chmap_get_ptr(map, key);
if (ptr) {
  *ptr = 200; // Modify in-place
}

chmap_construct(names, int, char *);
chmap_insert(names, key, "hello");
char *const *sptr = chmap_get_ptr(names, key);
if (sptr) {
  printf("%s\en", *sptr);  // Read the stored string
  (*sptr)[0] = 'j';         // Edit its bytes in place: "jello"
  chmap_insert(names, key, "a longer replacement");  // Replace it
}
chmap_destroy(map);
chmap_destroy(names);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_get (3),
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chmap_insert (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3)
